  {"type": "split", "photo": "in", "h2": "Heat pump installation in Scarborough without new ductwork",
   "html": "<p>Here's the good news about a Scarborough bungalow: the ducts are usually short, simple and already in place. That makes it a strong candidate for a ducted cold-climate heat pump. The outdoor unit takes the old air conditioner's spot, a new coil sits on top of the furnace, and the same ducts carry heat in winter and cool air in summer.</p>"
           "<p>Most owners keep the gas furnace as backup. The heat pump covers spring, fall and most of the winter, and the furnace takes over once a cold snap makes gas the better deal. One thermostat handles the switch. Enbridge gas-heated homes can still qualify for a heat pump rebate, commonly up to $2,000, when pre-approval is in place before installation.</p>"
           "<p>There's one catch we always check. Ducts sized for a 1960s furnace sometimes can't move enough air for a bigger coil, so we measure static pressure first. If a trunk or a few runs need work, you'll know before you sign, not halfway through the install day.</p>"
           "<p>Moving to a high-efficiency furnace changes the venting as well. The new furnace vents through PVC pipe out the side wall, and a gas water heater left alone in the old chimney may need a liner. We put that in the quote up front. For the rest of the city, including downtown semis and tight laneway lots, see our page on <a href=\"/toronto/\">HVAC in Toronto</a>.</p>"
           "<ul class=\"checklist\"><li>Coil and outdoor unit matched to the furnace blower</li><li>Static pressure tested before anything is sized</li><li>Permits, gas work and warranty registration included</li><li>Rebate pre-approval filed before install day</li></ul>"},
